# Topology, read over SSH at 2026-11-25T01:41Z–01:46Z (systemctl is-enabled / is-active / show,
# ss -ltn, grep, pgrep; nothing written).
topology: web droplet — rpc-tunnel-e.service enabled, active since 2026-11-14T06:36:10Z; ExecStart: ssh -N … -L 127.0.0.1:18545:127.0.0.1:8546 rpctunnel@<e>
topology: web droplet — 127.0.0.1:18545 is held by that ssh; the sale service's SALE_RPC_UPSTREAM=http://127.0.0.1:18545
topology: e — rand-node ExecStart carries --rpc 127.0.0.1:8545 and --public-rpc 127.0.0.1:8546; both bind loopback only
topology: e — rpctunnel's shell is /usr/sbin/nologin and its key carries permitopen="127.0.0.1:8546"
topology: only e runs the public listener — of the inventory's 26 hosts, 25 answered (a b c d e f lon1 sfo3 tor1 blr1 syd1 atl1 ams3 nyc1 nyc2 sfo2 mkc1 mem1 obs1 g1 g2 g3 g4 g5 g6): e has --public-rpc in its running rand-node, one listener on :8546 and one unit file naming it; each of the other 24 has no --public-rpc in the process, no RAND_NODE_PUBLIC_RPC in its environment, nothing listening on :8546 and no unit file naming either
topology: not read — archive2 (ssh timed out; the old account's host, not on chain 22)

# Open hazard (seen, not changed).
hazard: rpc-tunnel-obs1.service is still on the web droplet — disabled, inactive, never started since boot — and its ExecStart is still `-L 127.0.0.1:18545:127.0.0.1:8545 rpctunnel@<an address>`: the operator listener (--rpc, every viewing-key method answers a loopback caller), not :8546. The address is not obs1's in today's inventory; the unit and its pinned known_hosts name the one obs1 had before the 2026-10-22 rebuild. It binds the same local port as rpc-tunnel-e, so enabling it in e's place would put the public hop back on an operator listener with the sale proxy as the only gate (check 1 would then fail, naming the methods) — or on whoever holds that address. Repoint it to :8546 and obs1's current address (with permitopen="127.0.0.1:8546" and a public listener on obs1), or remove the unit, before it is ever enabled. obs1 has no public listener today.

# What this does not show: per-caller metering through the proxy, and the sale binary's commit
# (deploy/caddy/README.md "Which layer gates which method") — neither is part of this check.
